WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.319
Over the last ten years, Hollywood has clearly
figured out how to make big-screen superheroes

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:09.590
work, putting together incredibly successful
adaptations of characters and worlds that

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.370
comic fans love.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.420
Before that, though, well, let’s just call
it trial and error.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:19.169
The path to the MCU is littered with strange
efforts that have gone mostly forgotten since

00:00:19.169 --> 00:00:20.509
they were originally released.

00:00:20.509 --> 00:00:25.130
Here are the bizarre Marvel TV shows you've
probably never seen.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.269
Generation X (1996)

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:33.000
In an era when the X-Men franchise has a sprawling
series of movies and TV hits like The Gifted

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.180
and Legion, it's hard to believe that there
was a time in which Marvel’s mutants weren’t

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.180
a sure thing.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.870
In the ‘90s, the core team was thriving
in comics and ruling Saturday mornings with

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.760
a long-running animated series, and a spin-off
called Generation X featured a younger cast

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.640
of heroes, still learning the ropes.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.570
In a time before Buffy the Vampire Slayer
hit airwaves with a supernatural spin on a

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.679
similar idea, Generation X sounds like the
perfect way to bring Marvel’s edgy '90s

00:00:58.679 --> 00:01:00.499
teen drama to television.

00:01:00.499 --> 00:01:01.680
It wasn't.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.920
Filmed as a pilot and aired as a TV movie,
Generation X is a hallucinogenic nightmare,

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.350
and not the good kind.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:13.140
The production value is insanely low, the
acting is lousy, and somehow it doesn't even

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.110
manage to be entertainingly bad.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.920
Instead, it’s mostly just a reminder of
how lucky we are to have better stuff today.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.240
Silver Surfer (1998)

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.740
In the ‘90s, Marvel had huge success with
cartoons like X-Men and Spider-Man, but there

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.290
were plenty of other attempts at an animated
Marvel Universe that ended after a single

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.290
season.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:37.450
1998’s Silver Surfer animated series, however,
could’ve been remembered as one of the best.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.539
The Surfer is an inherently weird character,
a "cosmic sentinel" whose stories are often

00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:46.549
rife with themes of existentialism and philosophical
struggle.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:50.159
Rather than tone it down for the show and
turn it into a simple beat-'em-up about good

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.840
guys and bad guys, the animators leaned into
it with episodes about imperialism, slavery,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.010
and nihilism.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.851
It also incorporated some of the strangest
characters the cosmic side of the Marvel universe

00:02:00.851 --> 00:02:06.380
has to offer, including Gamora, Uatu the Watcher,
Eternity, and even Thanos.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.410
The art style was interesting, too, with imperfect
but charming CGI that looks looks like it's

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.390
ripped straight from the pages of a Jack Kirby
comic.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:19.000
Unfortunately, a legal dispute between Marvel
and Saban the production company, led to the

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.330
show's cancellation after a single season.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.390
It’s a shame, too - while it was one of
the strangest Marvel shows of all time, it

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.840
was also one of the most interesting.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.440
The Avengers: United They Stand (1999)

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.190
Looking back from a time when they’re in
the most popular movies ever made, it’s

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:39.820
hard to imagine a show about the Avengers
being so unpopular that it was canceled after

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:40.930
a single season.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.340
If you’ve ever seen Avengers: United They
Stand, however, you’ll understand exactly

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.010
why it got the axe.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.480
Rather than including popular heavy hitters
like Thor, Captain America, and Iron Man,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.760
the show was populated by heroes that were
B-listers at best.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.050
We're not knocking great characters like Hawkeye
and the Falcon, but Tigra and the Swordsman

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.930
aren't exactly the biggest names Marvel had
to offer.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:07.420
On the other hand, topping that team off with
a genuine stinker of a character in Wonder

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.720
Man is a recipe for disaster, and giving everyone
clunky, toyetic designs rather than their

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.470
iconic comic book costumes wasn’t a help,
either.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.470
Yikes.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.110
Blade: The Series (2006)

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.920
Let’s give credit where it’s due: Blade
effectively kickstarted the modern superhero

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.250
movie, and Blade II still holds up as one
of the best the genre has to offer.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:32.320
We’ll admit that Blade: Trinity might be
an imperfect conclusion to the trilogy, but

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.680
overall, Blade rules.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.880
With that in mind, it’s no surprise that
in 2006, Spike TV began developing a series

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.870
continuing the adventures of the legendary
"daywalker."

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:47.280
Unfortunately, the show lacked the budget,
production values, and star power of the big-screen

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.280
version.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.880
Wesley Snipes was absent, replaced by Sticky
Fingaz of the rap group Onyx, and none of

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.260
the other actors from the films appeared in
the series, either.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.950
It never quite felt like the Blade of the
movies, and ran for one very strange season

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:05.330
but before it ultimately got staked through
the heart after 13 episodes.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.010
Supaidaman (1978-1979)

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.420
There's an undeniable charm to Japanese tokusatsu
shows.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.280
The genre has a long tradition in Japan of
colorful costumed heroes fighting grotesque

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.109
monsters and giant robots, and America has
been importing it for decades now in the form

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:23.180
of the long-running Power Rangers franchise.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.790
All the more interesting, though, is the time
the reverse happened and Japan brought an

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.230
American hero to a tokusatsu show.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:35.000
As part of a licensing agreement, Toei, the
leading producer of tokusatsu in Japan, made

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.430
a show loosely based on Spider-Man in the
late ‘70s.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:43.300
Often transliterated as Supaidaman to distinguish
it from the original, Japan’s take resembles

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.180
its source material in name and costume only.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:51.379
Instead of Peter Parker, this Spidey is Takuya
Yamashiro, a daring motorcycle racer who got

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:55.020
his powers come from the blood of a dying
alien from Planet Spider.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.199
He's tasked with fighting a sinister interstellar
force called the Iron Cross Army, led by the

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.270
evil Professor Monster.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:05.139
Oh, and Spidey's got a giant robot called
Leopardon.

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:10.509
Supaidaman is all kinds of awesome if you're
into tokusatsu, and also super-weird thanks

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:15.360
to scenes where Supaidaman introduces himself
as an Emissary from Hell, or shoots at people

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.360
with a machine gun.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.190
But he also has a place in toku history: he
was the first live-action to introduce a hero

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:25.939
summoning a giant robot, which would become
a mainstay in Super Sentai and Power Rangers

00:05:25.939 --> 00:05:27.150
afterwards.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.509
Night Man (1997-1999)

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:34.289
The “Ultraverse” line is a pretty odd
slice of '90s comics.

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:38.960
These original superhero comics were published
by a company called Malibu, which was acquired

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:44.400
by Marvel in 1994 - but not before a few of
their heroes had been optioned for mass media.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.669
Along with a short-lived animated series about
a team called Ultraforce that was half-satire

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:56.360
and half-ripoff of the far more successful
X-Men, the major result was Night Man, which

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.490
ranks among the most peculiar superhero shows
of all time.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:05.919
The series focuses on a buff jazz saxophonist
who develops the telepathic ability to detect

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:10.289
evil after being struck by lightning, at the
cost of never being able to sleep.

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:14.810
With eight hours of his day freed up, he creates
a super-suit and starts fighting crime.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:15.940
At night.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:16.979
That last part is probably obvious.

00:06:16.979 --> 00:06:21.810
It's a stretch of a premise to begin with,
and coupling it with laughable production

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.910
values makes for a pretty freakish show.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:28.430
Somehow, it managed to last for two seasons,
and reeled in guest stars like Jerry Springer

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:30.090
and Donald Trump.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.529
A slice of pure '90s weirdness, Night Man
is mostly forgotten, but give the opening

00:06:34.529 --> 00:06:39.389
credits one watch, and we promise it's all
you're going to think about for a week.

00:06:39.389 --> 00:06:42.810
Mutant X (2001 - 2004)

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.610
Mutant X is the product of poor circumstance.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:50.889
To make a long story short, Marvel sold the
film and TV rights for the X-Men to 20th Century

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:51.889
Fox.

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:58.639
Years later, Marvel Studios producer Avi Arad
pushed Mutant X into production and was promptly

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:04.250
sued by Fox for violating their agreement
by putting X-Men characters in a TV show Fox

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:09.160
didn’t produce, which was a pretty big deal
after the success of the first X-Men movie.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:14.240
Because of this, changes were applied to distance
Mutant X from its source material, resulting

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:19.250
in a genuinely bizarre show that's not quite
an X-Men show but not quite not.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:23.870
The show is about a group of mutants who gained
their powers through a government experiment.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:27.949
The show follows them while they're on the
run, and being kept safe by one of the scientists

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:29.319
that helped create them.

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:33.770
The characters were unrelated to the X-Men,
none of them were allowed to have superheroic

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:38.960
codenames, and the only real resemblance to
the real X-Men is the presence of mutants,

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.969
and the team's mission is to find other mutants
and help keep them safe.

00:07:42.969 --> 00:07:47.400
Despite its general weirdness, the show ran
for three seasons before its production company

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:47.460
folded.

